    "summary": "Fiery ’79 show with epic Estimated Prophet→Eyes of the World & tight Shakedown Street",
    "blurb": "The second set kicks up a notch with an explosive Estimated Prophet into a marathon Eyes of the World, while the first set delivers a tight Shakedown Street → Samson & Delilah → High Time groove. Jerry’s guitar is on fire and Brent’s keyboards add distinct color throughout. The performance rounds out with a rousing U.S. Blues encore.",
    "review": "On November 29, 1979 at Cleveland Public Hall the Dead turned in a high‑octane fall run that still feels fresh for listeners today. The first set opened strong with an upbeat Alabama Getaway and a crisp Mexicali Blues before launching into a searing Shakedown Street that seamlessly segued into Samson & Delilah and High Time – a sequence reviewers call “tight” and full of punchy interplay. The second half truly ignites: Estimated Prophet rolls straight into a pre‑drum run of Eyes Of The World that stretches beyond twenty minutes, showcasing Jerry ripping his guitar with fast‑driven noodling while Brent Mydland’s keyboards weave bright, distinct textures. Phil Lesh sits upfront and center, driving the train with deep, articulate bass, and the rhythm section locks in solidly despite a stumble on Around & Around. The night caps off with an exuberant U.S. Blues encore that leaves the audience humming, cementing this show as one of the standout performances from the band’s late‑70s era.\n\nAcross all available soundboard sources the mix is crystal clear – especially the Miller recording (gd1979-11-29.sbd.miller.95831.sbeok.flac16) which captures every nuance, from the crisp cymbals to the warm depth of Phil’s bass and Brent’s synth tones. Whether you’re chasing the monster Eyes Of The World or just want a solid snapshot of the Dead’s fiery late‑fall energy, this show delivers it all in superb audio quality.",
